Sogen (KVM): a fault that exits as MMIO resumes where the hooks leave it

The KVM backend leaves a page protected to no access out of every memslot,
so a guest read of it does not fault in the guest: it exits as MMIO, with
the instruction half executed. handle_mmio_exit hands such an exit to the
memory violation hooks, and the Windows emulator's hook dispatches an
access violation -- RSP to the exception frame, RIP to
KiUserExceptionDispatcher -- and the loop goes back into KVM_RUN. But KVM
completes a pending MMIO access on that next KVM_RUN, and completing it
writes back the instruction's own next RIP. The dispatcher's RIP was lost
and its RSP kept: the guest went on after the faulting instruction, on the
exception frame's stack, and no handler ever ran.

A runtime whose GC stops threads by protecting a page meets it at once.
SBCL's Windows build polls such a page; under Sogen every poll after the
first faulted again (the handler that would unprotect it never ran), each
one cost the 0x600 bytes of an exception frame, and after 1362 faults the
2 MB stack sbcl.exe asks for was gone ("Failed to write KVM guest
memory"). Before that, code reading its stack slots read the frames Sogen
had zeroed: the nodecode build died on calls whose arguments came back 0.
Sogen's own log shows it: no syscall at all between one fault and the
next, and each fault's RSP the previous fault's frame.

abandon_pending_mmio finishes the access before the hooks run, by a
KVM_RUN with immediate_exit set (KVM's documented way to complete pending
operations without executing anything further), then puts back the
registers, segment registers and XSAVE state the completion changed. The
hooks see the faulting instruction, and the guest resumes where they
leave it. A real MMIO region's access still completes as before.

diff --git a/src/backends/kvm-emulator/kvm_x86_64_emulator.cpp b/src/backends/kvm-emulator/kvm_x86_64_emulator.cpp
index f5441dd..ba3dbee 100644
--- a/src/backends/kvm-emulator/kvm_x86_64_emulator.cpp
+++ b/src/backends/kvm-emulator/kvm_x86_64_emulator.cpp
@@ -2003,6 +2003,37 @@ namespace sogen::kvm
                 return std::nullopt;
             }
 
+            // A guest access to a page no memslot backs (one protected to no access) or a write to a read-only
+            // memslot exits as MMIO with its instruction half executed, and KVM finishes that instruction on the
+            // next KVM_RUN: it moves RIP past it whatever the registers were set to in between. A violation hook
+            // that dispatches an exception from that exit would have its RIP dropped and its RSP kept, so the
+            // guest went on after the faulting instruction on the dispatcher's stack -- never running the handler,
+            // and losing the exception frame's worth of stack each time. Finish the access now without running
+            // the guest (immediate_exit), and put back the faulting state it changed, so the hooks see the
+            // faulting instruction and the guest resumes where they leave it.
+            void abandon_pending_mmio()
+            {
+                const auto regs = this->get_regs();
+                const auto sregs = this->get_sregs();
+                const auto xsave = this->get_xsave();
+
+                // No host page backs the access: it reads zeros, and a write goes nowhere.
+                std::memset(this->run_->mmio.data, 0, sizeof(this->run_->mmio.data));
+                this->run_->immediate_exit = 1;
+                const auto rc = ::ioctl(this->vcpu_fd_.get(), KVM_RUN, 0);
+                const auto run_errno = errno;
+                this->run_->immediate_exit = this->stop_requested_ ? 1 : 0;
+                this->invalidate_register_cache();
+                if (rc >= 0 || run_errno != EINTR)
+                {
+                    throw std::runtime_error("KVM did not finish a pending MMIO access");
+                }
+
+                this->set_regs(regs);
+                this->set_sregs(sregs);
+                this->set_xsave(xsave);
+            }
+
             bool handle_mmio_exit()
             {
                 const auto& mmio = this->run_->mmio;
@@ -2026,6 +2057,7 @@ namespace sogen::kvm
                 const auto violation_address = translated_guest_address.value_or(mmio.phys_addr);
                 const auto violation_type = translated_guest_address ? memory_violation_type::protection : memory_violation_type::unmapped;
                 const auto operation = mmio.is_write ? memory_operation::write : memory_operation::read;
+                this->abandon_pending_mmio();
                 for (auto& [_, hook] : this->memory_violation_hooks_)
                 {
                     const auto result = hook(*this, violation_address, mmio.len, operation, violation_type);
